# Six notes; three are shown to the default reader (the double commutator as a Lyapunov exponent, the KAM theorem, the
# eigenvalue-one condition), two of them in the introduction. Listed in reading order.
BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-otoc", "after": "introduction-p1", "title": "The double commutator as a Lyapunov exponent", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Classical chaos is sensitivity to initial conditions: a small change $\delta q(0)$ grows as $e^{\lambda t}$, where $\lambda$ is the Lyapunov exponent. Equivalently the Poisson bracket $\{q(t),p(0)\}=\partial q(t)/\partial q(0)$ grows exponentially. The quantum counterpart replaces the bracket by a commutator $[U(t),W(0)]$; it is squared, so that phases do not cancel, and averaged in a thermal state, giving the double commutator. Expanding the square produces correlators whose operators are not time-ordered, often called out-of-time-order correlators. In a large-$N$ system the double commutator starts at order $1/N$ and grows as $e^{\lambda_Lt}/N$ until it is of order one, after a time of order $\log N/\lambda_L$. Maldacena, Shenker and Stanford proved the bound $\lambda_L\le2\pi/\beta$, which black holes and SYK at strong coupling saturate. The paper asks how this exponent leaves zero as interactions are switched on.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-marginal", "after": "introduction-p5", "title": "Relevant, marginal and exactly marginal couplings", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A CFT in $d$ spacetime dimensions deformed by $g\int d^dx\,\mathcal O$, with $\mathcal O$ of dimension $\Delta$, gives the coupling $g$ a mass dimension $d-\Delta$. If $\Delta<d$ the coupling is relevant: it grows towards the infrared and the theory flows to another fixed point. If $\Delta>d$ it is irrelevant. If $\Delta=d$ it is classically marginal, but quantum corrections usually generate a beta function at second order, $\beta_g\propto g^2$, set by the operator's three-point function with itself, so it becomes marginally relevant or irrelevant. A coupling whose beta function vanishes to all orders is exactly marginal, and its values form a conformal manifold of CFTs. Exact marginality is rare without supersymmetry. In SYK the random coupling is relevant, so its chaos is computed only at the infrared fixed point; the paper chooses exactly marginal random couplings to avoid the flow.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-kam", "after": "introduction-p7", "title": "The KAM theorem and the onset of classical chaos", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>An integrable Hamiltonian system with $n$ degrees of freedom has $n$ conserved quantities in involution, and its motion winds around invariant $n$-dimensional tori in phase space, quasi-periodically and without chaos. The Kolmogorov–Arnold–Moser (KAM) theorem says what happens when such a system is perturbed by a small, smooth term of strength $\epsilon$: tori whose frequencies are sufficiently irrational survive, slightly deformed, and they fill most of phase space, a fraction tending to one as $\epsilon\to0$. Chaos appears only in thin layers around destroyed resonant tori, and spreads over phase space only at larger perturbations. Chaos therefore does not take over as soon as integrability is broken; the onset of widespread chaos happens at a finite perturbation. The paper's discontinuous transition, with a quantum chaos exponent that stays zero up to a finite disorder strength, is what it compares with this classical result.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-syk", "after": "the-kernel-structure-of-the-four-point-function-p1", "title": "The SYK model at large N: self-energy and ladders", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In the SYK model, $N$ Majorana fermions interact through $J_{i_1\dots i_q}\chi_{i_1}\cdots\chi_{i_q}$ with Gaussian random couplings of variance $\propto J^2/N^{q-1}$. Averaging over the couplings ties pairs of interaction vertices together, and at large $N$ only “melonic” diagrams survive. The averaged propagator $G$ then obeys a closed Schwinger–Dyson equation, $G^{-1}=G_0^{-1}-\Sigma$ with self-energy $\Sigma=J^2G^{q-1}$; this can be packaged as the saddle point of a bilocal action in $G$ and $\Sigma$, the $G$–$\Sigma$ formalism. At order $1/N$ the connected four-point function is a sum of ladder diagrams, each rung adding two vertices joined by $q-2$ propagators: $\mathcal F=\sum_nK^n\mathcal F_0$. The paper keeps this structure around a general core CFT, where the free propagator is replaced by the core CFT and its higher correlators enter the self-energy and the kernel.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-eigenvalue", "after": "the-double-commutator-and-chaos-p2", "title": "The chaos exponent as a kernel eigenvalue", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>If the double commutator obeys a ladder equation $(1-K_R)W_R=F_0$, its late-time behaviour is governed by the kernel alone. The source $F_0$, a single rung, does not grow, while the ladder sums an unbounded number of rungs. A late-time growing piece of $W_R$ must therefore solve the homogeneous equation $K_RW_R=W_R$: it is an eigenfunction of the retarded kernel with eigenvalue one. Because the averaged theory is conformal, one can try a function of definite growth, $\propto e^{\lambda(t_1+t_2)/2}$, which the kernel maps to itself times an eigenvalue $k_R(\lambda)$. The chaos exponent is then the largest $\lambda$ with $k_R(\lambda)=1$. This is how $\lambda_L$ was computed in SYK by Kitaev and by Maldacena and Stanford, and the paper applies it to the retarded kernel of a general disordered CFT.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-minimal", "after": "disordered-minimal-models-p1", "title": "N=2 minimal models as Landau–Ginzburg theories", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A two-dimensional $\mathcal N=2$ theory of one chiral superfield $\Phi$ with superpotential $W=\Phi^q$ flows in the infrared to a superconformal fixed point, the $A_{q-1}$ minimal model, with central charge $c=3(q-2)/q$. A theory of chiral superfields defined by its superpotential in this way is called a Landau–Ginzburg theory. The superpotential is not renormalized and must have R-charge 2, which fixes the R-charge of $\Phi$ to $2/q$ and its scaling dimension to $1/q$. Its powers $\Phi^k$ with $k\le q-2$ form the chiral ring. A superpotential term built from $q$ chiral fields of dimension $1/q$ is classically marginal. Results of Green, Komargodski, Seiberg, Tachikawa and Wecht, and of Kol, show that, absent continuous non-R global symmetries, such terms are exactly marginal. For $q=3$, $c=1$ and the model is a free compact boson at a specific radius, so its chiral field is a vertex operator whose correlators are all known.</p>"""},
]
